{
  "result": "If I get to write about *whatever* I want, I want to write about small, ordinary wonders.\n\nNot the big ones \u2014 not black holes or pyramids or first kisses in the rain, though those are great too. I mean the tiny ones that almost don't count.\n\nLike the way morning light looks when it comes through a kitchen window and hits a glass of water and throws a little rainbow onto the table. Nobody put it there on purpose. Physics just did it for fun.\n\nOr secondhand bookstores. I love that someone, fifty years ago, underlined a sentence in pencil because it meant something to them on a Tuesday, and now you get to find it. You're holding a thought someone else couldn't let go of. That's time travel, but cheaper.\n\nOr the sound of a neighborhood at 6 a.m. before cars take over \u2014 birds arguing, a dog barking three streets away, someone's coffee maker gurgling. It's like the world clearing its throat before it starts talking.\n\nI think humans are really good at chasing the huge and missing the tiny. We want the vacation, the promotion, the big news, the plot twist. But most of life isn't plot. It's texture.\n\nToast that\u2019s *exactly* the right amount of crunchy. Finding a song that sounds like it was written from inside your own head. The smell of rain on hot pavement \u2014 petrichor, which is a beautiful word for something so simple. An old dog sighing in its sleep and twitching its paws like it's chasing something wonderful.\n\nMaybe joy isn't something you find at the end of a long search. Maybe it's something you practice noticing.\n\nSo if I had one wish for whoever's reading this, it's not a big wish. It's just: I hope today you notice one small thing you would have otherwise walked right past. A shadow shaped like something funny. A stranger laughing. The perfect fizz of a soda can opening.\n\nAnd I hope you let it count.\n\nWhat small wonder have you noticed lately?",
